# Coach speech (Slovak): the appraisal after the game (src/coach/appraisal.cpp).
# Same keys and variants as assets/coach/speech/en/appraisal.lang (its header documents every placeholder).
#
# Always positive, never blaming. The coach says "ty"; the player's gender is unknown: no second-person
# past tense or conditional ("vyhral si", "našiel si", "mohol si"), no adjective about the player
# ("pripravený"). Instead: nouns ("Výhra je tvoja"), the neuter impersonal ("podarilo sa ti", "dalo sa
# vziať", "neušlo ti to"), "we" ("remizovali sme", "vrátili sme"), the coach's own first person
# ("prehral som", "prišiel som o"), or the present tense ("{move} získava materiál").
# A move is never the subject of a past tense or an adjective (a queen move is spoken "dáma ..."): the
# noun "ťah" comes first ("ťah {move} priniesol"), or the neuter "lepšie bolo {crit_best}".
# Forms from common.lang: "v {n:ord_loc} ťahu" = "v 21. ťahu" (spoken "v dvadsať prvom ťahu"),
# "{n:ord} ťah" = "21. ťah", "do {crit_no:ord_gen} ťahu"; "{acc:percent}" = "85 %" (spoken "osemdesiat
# päť percent"); "{n:piece_acc}" = "jednu figúru"; "{m:check}" = "2 šachy"; "{n:best_move}" = "5
# najlepších ťahov"; "za {moves:move}" = "za 25 ťahov"; "{text:loc}" / "{text:acc}" = a phase in the
# locative / accusative (appraisal.phase.*.loc in common.lang). Counts after a colon are plain numbers
# ("Vzaté figúry: {n:f}", {n:f} says "jedna" / "dve" for the feminine "figúra").
# {level} is a level name, always in „...“ after a form of "úroveň" ("na úrovni „Začiatočník“").
# {opening} is the opening's citation name, always after a colon or alone, so it never needs a case.

# ---- Names used inside the lines ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phases: lower-case nominatives (otvorenie n, stredná hra f, koncovka f); no verb or adjective in the
# lines agrees with them.
